<h2>1. Fold styles and finished sizes (A4 / A3 flat)</h2>
<p>The first decision is not price, it is the pair of <em>flat size plus fold style</em> — together they set the press sheet area, the number of crease lines and the folding labour. These are the four formats that cover almost every job:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Fold style</th><th>Flat size</th><th>Finished size</th><th>Panels</th><th>Best used for</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Bi-fold (half fold)</td><td>A4 210×297mm</td><td>A5 148×210mm</td><td>4</td><td>Single offer, event flyers, restaurant menus</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tri-fold, letter fold (C-fold)</td><td>A4 210×297mm</td><td>DL 99×210mm</td><td>6</td><td>Street handouts, mailers that fit a DL envelope</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tri-fold, Z-fold (accordion)</td><td>A4 210×297mm</td><td>DL 99×210mm</td><td>6</td><td>Process steps, course schedules, price ladders</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Gate fold / double parallel</td><td>A3 297×420mm</td><td>A5 or DL</td><td>8</td><td>Maps, property packs, large event graphics</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Note that folding one A4 sheet into <strong>A5 (bi-fold)</strong> or into <strong>DL (tri-fold)</strong> uses exactly the same sheet area and the same paper cost. What changes is the <strong>number of creases (one for a bi-fold, two for a tri-fold) and the folding labour</strong>. So the common assumption that "tri-fold is much dearer than bi-fold" is mostly wrong — paper weight and quantity move the price far more. To compare finished sizes against flat flyers, read <a href="/en/blog/flyer-sizes-compared/">flyer sizes compared</a> and <a href="/en/blog/a5-vs-a6-flyer-size/">A5 vs A6 flyer size</a>.</p>

<h2>2. Paper and creasing: why folded leaflets must be scored</h2>
<p>The standard stocks are <strong>157gsm or 200gsm art paper</strong> (four-colour offset or digital), with a lighter 128gsm option selectable in the configurator. Heavier stock feels more substantial but cracks more readily along the fold.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Scoring / creasing:</strong> a steel rule presses a groove along the fold line before folding, so the fold sits crisp, does not spring back, and the coating does not split. Above roughly 157gsm — and on any laminated sheet — folding without scoring will almost always crack the print.</li>
  <li><strong>Grain direction:</strong> the fold line must run <strong>parallel to the paper grain</strong>. Folding across the grain lifts fibres, gives a ragged edge and can split heavier stock outright. This is the single most commonly missed point in supplied artwork.</li>
  <li><strong>Lamination (optional matte or gloss):</strong> matte reads as premium and kills glare; gloss gives stronger colour and better scuff resistance on street handouts. Either way the sheet still needs scoring.</li>
  <li><strong>Creep allowance:</strong> once you go beyond two folds, or use heavy stock, the innermost panel is pushed out by the accumulated thickness of the folded sheets. Narrowing that panel by 1-2mm keeps the finished piece sitting flat.</li>
</ul>

